  description:
    'A first-hand guide to the best things to do in La Paz, from Mi Teleférico and the city markets to Plaza Murillo, museums, viewpoints and Valle de la Luna.',
  path: '/best-things-to-do-in-la-paz/',
  ogImage: '/images/3-days-in-la-paz-itinerary/la-paz-city-panorama-1200w.jpg',
  photography: true,
};

const IMG = '/images/3-days-in-la-paz-itinerary';
const TELEFERICO = `${IMG}/la-paz-teleferico-over-city`;
const MARKET = `${IMG}/la-paz-central-market-street`;
const VALLE = `${IMG}/la-paz-valle-de-la-luna-landscape`;
const CITY = `${IMG}/la-paz-city-panorama`;
const STADIUM = `${IMG}/la-paz-hernando-siles-stadium`;
---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    route={['Bolivia', 'La Paz', 'South America']}
    label="Guide · Seven Experiences"
    title="Best Things to Do in La Paz"
    standfirst="La Paz is best understood through changing elevation and changing register: civic squares become market streets, museums give way to cable cars, and the urban bowl opens into dry, eroded landscape."
    stats={[
      { num: '7', label: 'Experiences' },
      { num: '3', label: 'Full days' },
      { num: '2', label: 'Markets' },
      { num: '1', label: 'City' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          La Paz is difficult to understand from street level. The centre gathers
          plazas, churches, museums and markets into a dense urban bowl, while brick
          neighbourhoods climb the slopes above it. Cable cars cross over roofs and
          roads, making the city’s vertical shape visible. Then the streets give way
          to the eroded formations of Valle de la Luna, where the city becomes a
          landscape rather than a collection of buildings.
        </p>
        <p>
          The strongest experiences here are the ones that explain those changes.
          Plaza Murillo gives the city a civic centre; the markets bring belief,
          produce and daily commerce into the same central streets; the museums hold
          older histories in rooms close enough to walk between. Mi Teleférico and
          Mirador Killi Killi make the geography legible from above, while Hernando
          Siles connects the city to football and public life. Taken together, they
          make La Paz more than a list of stops.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three full days give these layers room to sit beside one another. For the
          same places arranged as a dated journey, use the <a href="/3-days-in-la-paz-itinerary/">three-day La Paz itinerary</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="teleferico"
      number="I."
      name="Ride Mi Teleférico"
      epithet="Public transport above the city, and the clearest introduction to its vertical geography"
    />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Mi Teleférico is the best place to begin because it is both a practical way
        through La Paz and a changing view of the city. From a cabin, roofs, roads,
        steep hillsides and dense neighbourhoods share one frame. The city stops
        looking like a flat centre with an edge and starts to read as a basin built
        upwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        That double function matters. A cable car can shorten a crossing between
        parts of the city, but it also shows how those parts sit together. Streets
        disappear beneath the cabin; slopes that seemed distant become the next
        neighbourhood; the high plain and the lower bowl take their place in the
        same view. The specialist <a href="/mi-teleferico-la-paz/">Mi Teleférico in La Paz guide</a> goes further into the network. For a first visit, the useful rule is simple: use the system as transport rather than saving it for a single scenic ride.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${TELEFERICO}.jpg`}
      srcset={`${TELEFERICO}-480w.jpg 480w, ${TELEFERICO}-768w.jpg 768w, ${TELEFERICO}-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${TELEFERICO}-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${TELEFERICO}.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Mi Teleférico cabin crossing above the steep urban basin of La Paz."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Mi Teleférico turns the city’s steep geography into a way of moving through it."
      priority
    />

    <AtAGlance
      id="la-paz-glance"
      title="La Paz at a Glance"
      meta={[
        { label: 'Best first experience', value: 'Mi Teleférico, because it makes La Paz’s vertical geography understandable almost immediately.' },
        { label: 'Best central route', value: 'Plaza Murillo, San Francisco and the markets for civic life, belief and everyday commerce.' },
        { label: 'Best cultural depth', value: 'The museum cluster, where history, art and material culture sit within walking distance of the centre.' },
        { label: 'Best landscape contrast', value: 'Valle de la Luna, where the dense urban bowl gives way to dry, eroded formations.' },
      ]}
      daysHeading="Five Ways into La Paz"
      days={[
        { num: 'Vertical City', detail: 'Mi Teleférico · Killi Killi · Basin', text: 'The clearest way to understand La Paz. Cable cars and viewpoints reveal how neighbourhoods climb the slopes around the central bowl.' },
        { num: 'Civic Centre', detail: 'Plaza Murillo · San Francisco · Public life', text: 'The formal heart of the city, where government, religious architecture and central streets establish La Paz at ground level.' },
        { num: 'Markets', detail: 'Witches’ Market · Mercado Rodríguez', text: 'Two different readings of the city: ritual and Aymara cultural context on one side, produce and everyday commerce on the other.' },
        { num: 'Museums & History', detail: 'MUSEF · National Art · Coca Museum · Precious Metals', text: 'A compact cultural layer within the centre. Choose one or two rather than turning the day into a museum marathon.' },
        { num: 'Landscape & Public Life', detail: 'Valle de la Luna · Hernando Siles', text: 'Two very different edges to the guide: eroded landscape outside the centre and football as part of the city’s collective life.' },
      ]}
    />

    <Chapter
      id="central"
      number="II."
      name="Walk Plaza Murillo and Central La Paz"
      epithet="Civic buildings, old streets and the public face of the city"
    />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Plaza Murillo gives central La Paz its formal centre. The square is named
        for Pedro Domingo Murillo, one of the leaders associated with the 1809
        uprising in Upper Peru, and its surrounding buildings keep that political
        history close to the present. The Metropolitan Cathedral, government
        buildings and the traffic of the surrounding streets make the square a place
        to read the city’s public life, not simply to pass through on the way to the
        markets.
      </p>
      <p>
        From there, the central walk can widen through Sucre Square, the streets
        around San Francisco and the museum district. Colonial religious buildings,
        civic façades and the slopes beyond them occupy the same short sequence. It
        is a good counterweight to the cable cars: first see how La Paz rises above
        the centre, then spend time in the streets where its institutions meet.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="markets"
      number="III."
      name="Walk the Witches’ Market and Mercado Rodríguez"
      epithet="Two market districts where belief and everyday commerce sit within the city’s centre"
    />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The Witches’ Market is not a single grand market hall but a set of shops and
        stalls around Calle Linares. Offerings associated with Pachamama sit beside
        herbs, candles, amulets, crafts and other goods. The place is most useful
        when approached as part of La Paz’s living culture rather than as a stage set
        for unusual photographs. Look closely, move respectfully and let the street
        remain a working place for the people who use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mercado Rodríguez gives the day a different emphasis. It is one of the
        city’s emblematic markets, with produce, vendors and the ordinary business of
        buying and selling. The contrast between the two areas is not a neat division
        between ritual and reality; both belong to the city’s working centre. The
        dedicated <a href="/la-paz-markets/">La Paz markets guide</a> keeps the two visits together and gives the practical sequence more room.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${MARKET}.jpg`}
      srcset={`${MARKET}-480w.jpg 480w, ${MARKET}-768w.jpg 768w, ${MARKET}-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${MARKET}-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${MARKET}.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Market street in central La Paz with stalls and everyday foot traffic."
      width={2048}
      height={1152}
      caption="Market streets bring everyday commerce into the centre of La Paz."
    />

    <Chapter
      id="valle"
      number="IV."
      name="Visit Valle de la Luna"
      epithet="A separate morning where the city gives way to eroded formations"
    />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Valle de la Luna earns a place because it changes the scale of the visit.
        After plazas, museums, market streets and cable cars, the route leaves the
        dense urban bowl for formations cut into pale, dry earth. The paths move
        between spires and ridges rather than between buildings, and the surrounding
        city becomes something seen beyond the landforms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Give it a separate block rather than trying to fit it between central stops.
        The transfer is short enough to make the excursion practical, but the point
        is the change of setting. The <a href="/valle-de-la-luna-la-paz/">Valle de la Luna guide</a> is the better place to settle questions about the visit; here, the recommendation is simply to let La Paz have a landscape chapter of its own.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${VALLE}.jpg`}
      srcset={`${VALLE}-480w.jpg 480w, ${VALLE}-768w.jpg 768w, ${VALLE}-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${VALLE}-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${VALLE}.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Valle de la Luna formations and dry landscape near La Paz."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Valle de la Luna carries the visit beyond the dense city and into eroded land."
    />

    <Chapter
      id="museums"
      number="V."
      name="Spend Time in the Museums"
      epithet="History, art and material culture within walking distance of the centre"
    />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        La Paz’s museum district works best as a cluster. The Coca Museum, MUSEF,
        the National Art Museum and the Museum of Precious Metals each offer a
        different way into Bolivia’s history and culture. None needs to carry the
        whole day alone. Together they make the centre less like a sequence of
        viewpoints and more like a place with deep material history.
      </p>
      <p>
        San Francisco belongs naturally in the same central walk. Its church and
        surrounding complex connect religious history with the streets and markets
        outside, while the nearby collections provide a wider frame. Choose the
        museum that most interests you, then leave enough time to walk between the
        buildings. The value is in the movement from public square to collection,
        not in rushing through every door.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="killi-killi"
      number="VI."
      name="Look Out from Mirador Killi Killi"
      epithet="A city-wide view after the city has been walked at street level"
    />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Mirador Killi Killi is most useful after the centre has become familiar. From
        above, La Paz gathers itself into one broad image: the bowl, the roads, the
        brick slopes and the mountains beyond. The view does not replace the markets
        or museums. It gives them a geography.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the viewpoint to choose when the question is how the city fits
        together. Mi Teleférico shows the urban fabric in motion; Killi Killi holds
        it still. After both, the steepness of La Paz is no longer an abstract fact
        but the organising feature of the city.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${CITY}.jpg`}
      srcset={`${CITY}-480w.jpg 480w, ${CITY}-768w.jpg 768w, ${CITY}-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${CITY}-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${CITY}.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="La Paz spread across its basin and surrounding slopes from an elevated viewpoint."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="From above, La Paz reads as roofs, roads and neighbourhoods climbing the basin."
    />

    <Chapter
      id="stadium"
      number="VII."
      name="See Estadio Hernando Siles"
      epithet="Football, public space and the city’s high-altitude civic geography"
    />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Estadio Hernando Siles adds a different kind of public space to the list. It
        is one of Bolivia’s major football venues, set within the city rather than
        separated from it as a distant attraction. Even from outside, the stadium
        belongs to the same geography as the plazas, markets and cable cars: a place
        where the city gathers around a shared institution.
      </p>
      <p>
        The visit does not need a match to justify the stop. The exterior and its
        urban setting are enough to make football part of the way La Paz is read. It
        is a useful final experience because it returns the journey from wide views
        and dry landscape to a building made for collective attention.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${STADIUM}.jpg`}
      srcset={`${STADIUM}-480w.jpg 480w, ${STADIUM}-768w.jpg 768w, ${STADIUM}-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${STADIUM}-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${STADIUM}.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Exterior of Estadio Hernando Siles in La Paz."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Estadio Hernando Siles, where football joins La Paz’s public geography."
    />

    <Practical place="La Paz — Things to Do" detail="Seven experiences · three days" detailIcon="🧭">
      <PracticalSection label="Shortest route to a great first visit" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>One day</strong> — Plaza Murillo, the Witches’ Market, Mi Teleférico and Mirador Killi Killi. This gives civic La Paz, market life and the city’s vertical geography in one sequence.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Two days</strong> — Keep the central route on day one, then choose museums plus Mercado Rodríguez or Valle de la Luna depending on whether culture or landscape matters more.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Three days</strong> — Follow the <a href="/3-days-in-la-paz-itinerary/">complete La Paz itinerary</a>, giving Valle de la Luna its own block and leaving enough room for markets, museums and cable-car movement.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>If you only choose three things</strong> — Mi Teleférico, the central markets and one elevated city view.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
      <PracticalSection label="What each part of La Paz does best" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Geography:</strong> Mi Teleférico and Mirador Killi Killi.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Civic history:</strong> Plaza Murillo and central La Paz.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Markets and daily life:</strong> Witches’ Market and Mercado Rodríguez.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Culture:</strong> Museum district and San Francisco.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Landscape:</strong> Valle de la Luna.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
      <PracticalSection label="Watch-outs" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Do not treat Mi Teleférico as only a scenic ride.</strong> It works best when used as part of the route through the city.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Keep Valle de la Luna separate from a packed central day.</strong> Its value is the change of landscape, not squeezing it between urban stops.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Choose museums rather than collecting them.</strong> One or two strong visits are more useful than rushing every collection.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Respect the markets as working places.</strong> Photograph thoughtfully and avoid turning people or ritual goods into scenery.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="priorities" rule={false}>What to prioritise with one or two days</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        With one day, keep the central sequence compact: Plaza Murillo, the Witches’
        Market, Mi Teleférico and Mirador Killi Killi. It moves from civic space to
        market street, then uses the cable cars and viewpoint to show how the city
        rises around them.
      </p>
      <p>
        With two days, give one day to the centre and markets, then choose between
        the museum cluster and Valle de la Luna according to whether history or the
        landscape matters more. Add Hernando Siles if football and public space are
        part of the interest. With three days, follow the fuller <a href="/3-days-in-la-paz-itinerary/">La Paz itinerary</a>, which gives Valle de la Luna its own day and keeps the movement between central stops manageable.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="planning" rule={false}>Practical planning</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        La Paz works best when transport and walking are treated as part of the
        sightseeing. Use Mi Teleférico where it connects the day, stay central if the
        markets and museums matter, and avoid forcing Valle de la Luna into a short
        gap between urban stops. The exact order can change; the contrast between
        the settings is the important part.
      </p>
      <p>
        Altitude shaped the plan through walking and cable cars rather than harder
        training. The traveller had already spent time at altitude before La Paz,
        but individual responses differ. Keep the first walks manageable, allow
        extra time for climbs and seek medical advice if symptoms are concerning.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the markets, photograph the street rather than treating people or ritual
        objects as scenery. For museums, choose a small group rather than trying to
        cover every collection. Those choices leave enough space for the city to be
        read rather than merely completed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="closing" rule={false}>The City in Layers</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The cable cars explain La Paz’s vertical shape. The markets explain how
        belief and daily commerce share the centre. Plaza Murillo explains civic
        power; the museums give history material form; Valle de la Luna carries the
        city into a different landscape. Killi Killi gathers the geography into one
        view, and Hernando Siles brings the attention back to public life.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why the best things to do in La Paz are not interchangeable stops.
        They move between elevation, history, commerce, landscape and collective
        space until the city begins to hold together. The <a href="/3-days-in-la-paz-itinerary/">three-day itinerary</a> is the natural next step when those layers need to become a route.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="beat">But the road is ever onward.</p>

    <FurtherReading
      label="Continue through La Paz"
      cards={[
        { href: '/3-days-in-la-paz-itinerary/', region: 'Bolivia · La Paz', title: '3 Days in La Paz: Complete Itinerary', excerpt: 'The full La Paz sequence through markets, museums, Mi Teleférico, Valle de la Luna and the city edge.', cta: 'READ CHRONICLE' },
